issue.body:
It seems that looping over a list of mixed derived and base classes makes `mypyc` crash.

Here is a (minimal?) reproducer. For the failure, execute:
> docker buildx build --progress=plain --tag test --target=failure - < dockerfile

and to see it succeed, execute:
> docker buildx build --progress=plain --tag test --target=success - < dockerfile

It seems that the crash is during the processing of the `for obj in self.objs` (at least, commenting it out in the *failure* case "fixes" it).

The fix is to go from:
```python
self.objs: typing.Final[list[DerivedLevel1 | DerivedLevel2A | DerivedLevel2B]] = ...
```
to
```python
self.objs: typing.Final[list[DerivedLevel1]] = ...
```

```dockerfile
FROM python:3.12 AS base

RUN pip install mypy[mypyc]==1.19.1

FROM base as failure

COPY <<EOF test_failure.py
import abc
import typing

class Base(abc.ABC):
    @abc.abstractmethod
    def foo(self, value: int) -> int | None:
        pass

class DerivedLevel1(Base):
    @typing.override
    def foo(self, value: int) -> int | None:
        return value > 42

class DerivedLevel2A(DerivedLevel1):
    pass

class DerivedLevel2B(DerivedLevel1):
    pass

class Confused(Base):
    def __init__(self) -> None:
        self.objs: typing.Final[list[DerivedLevel1 | DerivedLevel2A | DerivedLevel2B]] = [
            DerivedLevel2B(),
            DerivedLevel2A(),
            DerivedLevel1(),
        ]

    @typing.override
    def foo(self, value: int) -> int | None:
        for obj in self.objs:
            if (candidate := obj.foo(value)) is not None:
                return candidate
        return None

if __name__ == '__main__':
    Confused().foo(value=42)
EOF

RUN mypyc --strict --verbose test_failure.py

FROM base AS success

COPY <<EOF test_success.py
import abc
import typing

class Base(abc.ABC):
    @abc.abstractmethod
    def foo(self, value: int) -> int | None:
        pass

class DerivedLevel1(Base):
    @typing.override
    def foo(self, value: int) -> int | None:
        return value > 42

class DerivedLevel2A(DerivedLevel1):
    pass

class DerivedLevel2B(DerivedLevel1):
    pass

class Confused(Base):
    def __init__(self) -> None:
        self.objs: typing.Final[list[DerivedLevel1]] = [
            DerivedLevel2B(),
            DerivedLevel2A(),
            DerivedLevel1(),
        ]

    @typing.override
    def foo(self, value: int) -> int | None:
        for obj in self.objs:
            if (candidate := obj.foo(value)) is not None:
                return candidate
        return None

if __name__ == '__main__':
    Confused().foo(value=42)
EOF

RUN mypyc --strict --verbose test_success.py
```

</details>

When a class method calls a class method of a native base class through `super()`, the base method gets
`type(cls)` (the metaclass) as `cls` instead of the class.

```python
class A:
    @classmethod
    def make(cls) -> str:
        return cls.__name__


class B(A):
    @classmethod
    def make(cls) -> str:
        return "B:" + super().make()


print(B.make())
```

```
$ python repro.py
B:B
$ mypyc repro.py && python -c "import repro"
B:type
```

Nothing fails in this example. The base method just runs with `cls` set to `type`.
